<section class="landing-section landing-section--alt">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Why numbers trip you up in another language</h2>
    <p>The digits are the same everywhere — but saying them out loud follows different rules, and that's where you stall.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The grouping is different</strong> — English breaks numbers every three digits (thousand · million · billion), while Korean breaks every four (만 <em>man</em> · 억 <em>eok</em> · 조 <em>jo</em>). "48,500" is "forty-eight thousand five hundred" but "사만 팔천오백" in Korean.</li>
      <li><strong>Ordinals are irregular</strong> — first, second, third, plus the 11th/12th/13th exceptions and when to add -st/-nd/-rd/-th.</li>
      <li><strong>Dates, times and fractions are set phrases</strong> — "the first of April", "o'clock", "three fifths" all look different from the digits.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This app lets you <strong>see it and hear it at the same time</strong>, in both languages. No sign-up — open it and start.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Five categories, two ways — Once · Auto</h2>
    <img src="/assets/images/englishnumber/screenshots/en/01_main_menu.png" alt="Home screen with Numbers, Ordinals, Fraction, Date and Time categories, each with Once and Auto buttons" class="app-screenshot">
    <p>Pick one of five categories from the home screen, then listen in one of two ways.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Numbers</strong> (cardinals) · <strong>Ordinals</strong> · <strong>Fraction</strong> · <strong>Date</strong> · <strong>Time</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Once</strong> — type any value and hear it read once</li>
      <li><strong>Auto</strong> — let the number count up on its own and listen on repeat</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The top of the screen shows whether the <strong>voice data</strong> needed for pronunciation is installed, so you always know you're ready.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section landing-section--alt">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Hear it both ways, on repeat — Auto</h2>
    <img src="/assets/images/englishnumber/screenshots/en/02_number_auto.png" alt="Numbers Auto screen showing 48,500 as '사만 팔천오백 · forty-eight thousand five hundred' with increment, repeat and language chips" class="app-screenshot">
    <p>Every value appears <strong>in Korean and English on one line</strong> — for example 48,500 shows as "사만 팔천오백 · forty-eight thousand five hundred." In <strong>Once</strong> mode you can also break a big number into digit groups so you hear exactly where it splits at million and thousand.</p>
    <p><strong>Auto</strong> is not a quiz — it's relaxed, repeated listening. Set a starting value, press play, and the number climbs on its own while the voice keeps up.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Step</strong> — +1, +10, +100, +thousand … up to billions</li>
      <li><strong>Repeat</strong> — 5, 15, 65 or 115 times</li>
      <li><strong>Language</strong> — English always on; turn on <strong>Korean</strong> to hear Korean then English</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Ordinals — first, second … and 11th, 12th, 13th</h2>
    <img src="/assets/images/englishnumber/screenshots/en/03_ordinal.png" alt="Ordinals screen showing 45,200th" class="app-screenshot">
    <p>Ordinals are full of exceptions. The app attaches <strong>-st / -nd / -rd / -th</strong> correctly, handles first · second · third and the tricky <strong>11th · 12th · 13th</strong>, and reads large ordinals such as 45,200th too.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section landing-section--alt">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Fractions — three fifths</h2>
    <img src="/assets/images/englishnumber/screenshots/en/04_fraction.png" alt="Fraction screen with natural number, numerator and denominator fields" class="app-screenshot">
    <p>Enter a fraction as three parts — <strong>natural number · numerator · denominator</strong>. It follows the English rule of cardinal-over-ordinal, so "3/5" is read as "three fifths," and mixed numbers add <strong>and</strong>, as in "8 and 3/5." The Korean reading ("5분의 3") is shown alongside.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Dates — the first of April twenty twenty-six</h2>
    <img src="/assets/images/englishnumber/screenshots/en/05_date.png" alt="Date screen with 2026-07-03 and a calendar picker" class="app-screenshot">
    <p>Type a date or pick it from the <strong>calendar</strong>, and it's read in English as "the &lt;ordinal&gt; of &lt;month&gt; &lt;year&gt;." The <strong>year</strong> is the hard part, and it's read the natural way: 2026 as "twenty twenty-six," 2010 as "twenty ten," 2000 as "two thousand." The Korean reading is shown next to it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section landing-section--alt">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Time — 3 o'clock</h2>
    <img src="/assets/images/englishnumber/screenshots/en/06_time.png" alt="Time screen with 07:01 and a clock picker" class="app-screenshot">
    <p>Enter a time or pick it from the <strong>clock</strong>. On the hour it reads "o'clock," otherwise it reads the hour and minutes in sequence. Leave it on <strong>Auto</strong> to step forward by <strong>minute</strong> or <strong>hour</strong> and hear time expressions on repeat.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>Fine-tune the voice — Settings</h2>
    <img src="/assets/images/englishnumber/screenshots/en/07_settings.png" alt="Settings screen with voice pitch, voice speed and listening interval sliders" class="app-screenshot">
    <p>Adjust the reading until it sits comfortably in your ear.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Voice pitch</strong> — a little lower / normal / a little higher</li>
      <li><strong>Voice speed</strong> — five steps, from slow to very fast</li>
      <li><strong>Listening interval</strong> — the pause before the next number: long / normal / short</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="landing-section landing-section--alt">
  <div class="landing-section__inner">
    <h2>What you need — device voices (TTS)</h2>
    <p>Pronunciation uses your device's <strong>text-to-speech (TTS) engine</strong>, so both <strong>Korean and English voice data</strong> need to be installed. If a voice is missing, the app points you to the install screen and jumps straight to your system TTS settings or the Google voice install. You can check the status any time at the top of the home screen.</p>
  </div>
</section>

